{"name":"Rabbit’s Foot","hex":"#d2c1b5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rabbit","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mammal of most genera of the family Leporidae, with long ears, long hind legs and a short, fluffy tail. (Those of the larger kind are instead called hares)","The meat from this animal."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hunt rabbits.","To flee."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rabbit"},{"term":"foot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A biological structure found in many animals that is used for locomotion and that is frequently a separate organ at the terminal part of the leg.","Specifically, a human foot, which is found below the ankle and is used for standing and walking."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To use the foot to kick (usually a ball).","To pay (a bill)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/foot"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rabbit's foot","blocks":[{"text":"In some cultures, a rabbit's foot is carried as an amulet believed to bring good luck. This belief is held by people in a great number of places around the world, including Europe, Africa, Australia and North and South America. In variations of this superstition, the rabbit it came from must possess certain attributes, such as having been killed in a particular place, using a particular method, or by a person possessing particular attributes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Rabbitsfoot.jpg/330px-Rabbitsfoot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabbit's_foot"},{"title":"Foot","blocks":[{"text":"The foot is an anatomical structure found in many vertebrates. It is the terminal portion of a limb which bears weight and allows locomotion. In many animals with feet, the foot is an organ at the terminal part of the leg made up of one or more segments or bones, generally including claws and/or nails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Foot_on_white_background.jpg/330px-Foot_on_white_background.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foot"},{"title":"Rabbit","blocks":[{"text":"Rabbits, or bunnies, are small mammals in the family Leporidae, which is in the order Lagomorpha. They are familiar throughout the world as a small herbivore, a prey animal, a domesticated form of livestock, and a pet, having a widespread effect on ecologies and cultures. The most widespread rabbit genera are Oryctolagus and Sylvilagus. The former, Oryctolagus, includes the European rabbit, Oryctolagus cuniculus, which is the ancestor of the hundreds of breeds of domestic rabbit and has been introduced on every continent except Antarctica. The latter, Sylvilagus, includes over 13 wild rabbit species, among them the cottontails and tapetis. Wild rabbits not included in Oryctolagus and Sylvilagus include several species of limited distribution, including the pygmy rabbit, volcano rabbit, and Sumatran striped rabbit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Oryctolagus_cuniculus_Rcdo.jpg/330px-Oryctolagus_cuniculus_Rcdo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabbit"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/0d/Rabbit_foot_blues.jpg/500px-Rabbit_foot_blues.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/0d/Rabbit_foot_blues.jpg/500px-Rabbit_foot_blues.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The cover to \"Rabbit Foot Blues\", a blues song by Blind Lemon Jefferson, links the rabbit's foot tradition with the bones of the dead.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARabbit_foot_blues.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/MedialViewOfRightFoot.png/500px-MedialViewOfRightFoot.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/MedialViewOfRightFoot.png/500px-MedialViewOfRightFoot.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Surface anatomy of the right foot and ankle; medial aspect. 1, tibialis anterior tendon — 2, medial malleolus — 3, tibialis posterior tendon — 4, navicular tuberosity — 5, flexor digitorum longus tendon — 6, sustentaculum tali of calcaneus — 7, flexor hallucis longus tendon — 8, Achilles tendon — 9, head of talus — 10, joint between navicular and first cuneiform — 11, joint between first metatarsal and first cuneiform — 12, metatarso-phalangeal joint. (After Rawlings.)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMedialViewOfRightFoot.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Newborn-Baby-Feet.jpg/500px-Newborn-Baby-Feet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Newborn-Baby-Feet.jpg/1280px-Newborn-Baby-Feet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Newborn-Baby-Feet.jpg/500px-Newborn-Baby-Feet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The feet of a newborn infant","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANewborn-Baby-Feet.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Metti_%28cropped%29.JPG/500px-Metti_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Metti_%28cropped%29.JPG/500px-Metti_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A woman's foot, decorated with nail polish and henna, and wearing a metti (toe ring) on the second toe, for her wedding","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMetti_(cropped).JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Blausen_0411_FootAnatomy.png/500px-Blausen_0411_FootAnatomy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Blausen_0411_FootAnatomy.png/500px-Blausen_0411_FootAnatomy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration of bones in lower leg and foot","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlausen_0411_FootAnatomy.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Gray437.png/120px-Gray437.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Gray437.png/250px-Gray437.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Gray437.png/120px-Gray437.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Anterior leg muscles","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray437.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Gray438.png/120px-Gray438.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Gray438.png/250px-Gray438.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Gray438.png/120px-Gray438.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray438.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Gray439.png/120px-Gray439.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https:/